---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro'
import AssertionCard from '../components/AssertionCard.astro'
import ChartFigure from '../components/ChartFigure.astro'
import { assertion, nodes } from '../lib/feeds'
import { bar } from '../lib/chart-spec'

const raid = assertion('the-night-the-jail-was-taken')
const succession = assertion('the-office-passed-from-father-to-son')
const bank = assertion('the-man-in-the-cell-was-charged-with-an-allen-county-bank')
const tanks = assertion('the-county-had-two-tank-installations')
const books = assertion('the-war-turned-the-locomotive-works-books')
const railTown = assertion('lima-was-a-railroad-town-before-an-oil-town')
const noTrains = assertion('no-passenger-train-calls-in-this-county')
const wentToWar = assertion('one-person-in-ten-went-to-war')
const warDead = assertion('the-roster-never-totalled-its-own-dead')
const corinth = assertion('more-died-at-corinth-than-anywhere')

const booksSpec = bar(books.figures.map((f) => f.label), books.figures.map((f) => f.value), {
  yTitle: 'Locomotives ordered',
  labelValues: true,
})

const causeSpec = bar(
  warDead.figures.map((f) => f.label),
  warDead.figures.map((f) => f.value),
  { yTitle: 'Men (a floor)', labelValues: true },
)

const corinthSpec = bar(
  corinth.figures.map((f) => f.label),
  corinth.figures.map((f) => f.value),
  { yTitle: 'Men (a floor)', labelValues: true },
)

const crossingSpec = bar(
  noTrains.figures.map((f) => f.label),
  noTrains.figures.map((f) => f.value),
  { yTitle: 'Open crossings', labelValues: true },
)

// The spine is read out of the graph, never typed here. A page that lists the county's dated
// record by hand would keep saying "1885" long after a phase had added something later, and the
// gap this page is largely about would be the first thing to go stale.
const year = (s: string | undefined) => (s ? Number(s.slice(0, 4)) : Number.NaN)

const events = nodes
  .filter((n) => n.class === 'event' && !Number.isNaN(year(n.properties.occurred)))
  .map((n) => ({ node: n, at: year(n.properties.occurred), on: n.properties.occurred }))
  .toSorted((a, b) => a.at - b.at)

const periods = nodes
  .filter((n) => n.class === 'period' && !Number.isNaN(year(n.properties.began)))
  .map((n) => ({ node: n, from: year(n.properties.began), to: year(n.properties.ended) }))
  .toSorted((a, b) => a.from - b.from)

// The longest run of years with no dated event in it, computed from the same list the table
// renders. Written as a pair so the prose below can name both ends without repeating itself.
const gaps = events.slice(1).map((e, i) => ({ from: events[i].at, to: e.at }))
const widest = gaps.reduce((a, b) => (b.to - b.from > a.to - a.from ? b : a), gaps[0])
const last = events[events.length - 1].at
const since = new Date().getUTCFullYear() - last
---

<Base
  title="History"
  lede="Everything this corpus can date, in order — and the stretches between."
>
  <section>
    <h2>The county's dated record, and the hole in the middle of it</h2>
    <p>
      A civic reference should be able to say when things happened. This one can say it {
        events.length
      } times. The list below is not a history of Allen County; it is the subset of the county's
      history that this corpus has put a date on from a source it can cite, which is a much
      smaller thing and is shown here at its real size.
    </p>
    <table>
      <caption>Dated events in the corpus, earliest first</caption>
      <thead>
        <tr><th scope="col">Date</th><th scope="col">Event</th><th scope="col">Kind</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        {
          events.map((e) => (
            <tr>
              <td>{e.on}</td>
              <td>{e.node.label}</td>
              <td>{e.node.properties.event_type ?? '—'}</td>
            </tr>
          ))
        }
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p>
      The gaps are the point. The widest run with nothing dated in it is {widest.to - widest.from}
      years, from {widest.from} to {widest.to}; the longest of all is the {since} years since {last},
      which is where this corpus stops being able to name a day at all. That is not because nothing
      happened. It is because the corpus spent most of its phases counting rather than dating, and a
      count has no date on it — it has a year and a table.
    </p>
    <p>
      Between the oil strike and the raid the same pattern holds in reverse. The corpus can tell you
      that Lima's manufacturing workforce grew 82 per cent between 1899 and 1909 and that the county
      reached 56,580 people in 1910, and it cannot tell you the day either thing happened, because
      neither is the kind of thing that has one. What it has known across those years is what Allen
      County <em>was</em>. What happened in them is newer work.
    </p>
    <p>
      The most recent entry came from a land record rather than from a newspaper, and that is worth
      noticing about this list. A conveyance is dated to the day whether or not anybody reported it,
      and the county has abstracted its conveyances by piece of ground since the federal patents. A
      corpus that wants to know when things happened here has been reading the wrong kind of source.
    </p>
    <p>
      One entry on this list was added because the list existed. The 1918 renaming of German
      Township had been established two phases earlier, with the day and the petition's own
      language, and recorded as prose on a place and on a question — which is where an answer goes
      and not where a happening goes. It was missing from every view built on the event class, and
      the stretch after 1885 was reading fifteen years emptier than the corpus's own knowledge.
      Publishing found it; reading had not.
    </p>
    <p>
      The named spans do reach across it, because a period is a claim about a stretch rather than
      about a day:
    </p>
    <table>
      <caption>Named periods in the corpus</caption>
      <thead>
        <tr><th scope="col">Span</th><th scope="col">Period</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        {
          periods.map((p) => (
            <tr>
              <td>{p.from}–{Number.isNaN(p.to) ? 'present' : p.to}</td>
              <td>{p.node.label}</td>
            </tr>
          ))
        }
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Why Lima is where it is</h2>
    <p>
      This site has told the county's industrial story as an oil story since the population page
      was written, and the oil is not where it starts. Two railroads chose to cross at Lima —
      one in 1854 and one four years later — and the canal towns fifteen miles west, which had
      the head start, did not get a crossing.
    </p>
    <AssertionCard assertion={railTown} />
    <p>
      The county paid $50,000 in stock for the first of them and later sold the stock at par, so
      the road cost it nothing. In 1872 Lima and Ottawa Township voted on $100,000 of bonds for
      two more lines: something over 700 votes were cast and eight of them were against. By 1883
      there were about 102 miles of main track in the county, and by 1906 two railroad shops in
      Lima employed more than five hundred men each.
    </p>
    <p>
      One date on the table above is not a date so much as a range. Three county histories name
      the same first railroad and give three different years — 1853, 1854 and 1856 — and none of
      them cites the record it read. The corpus takes 1854, because it is the nearest witness's
      year and because the same book that dates the opening latest also reports that the
      locomotive used to build the line arrived in 1854, by canal, as freight to Delphos. A
      construction engine delivered in 1854 cannot serve a line already open in 1853.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>And what is left of it</h2>
    <AssertionCard assertion={noTrains} />
    <ChartFigure
      id="crossings"
      title="Open highway-rail crossings by place, 2026"
      caption="FRA Highway-Rail Grade Crossing Inventory. A crossing is a place a road meets a track, so this counts encounters and not miles; the corpus has no modern track mileage to set against the 102 miles of 1883."
      columns={['Place', 'Open crossings']}
      rows={noTrains.figures.map((f) => [f.label, String(f.value)])}
    />
    <script type="application/json" data-chart-spec="crossings" set:html={JSON.stringify(crossingSpec)} is:inline />
    <p>
      Two of the five are Class I railroads and three are short lines. The busiest is CSX, at up to
      24 trains a day and 60 miles an hour through Lima; the one with the most crossings is the
      Chicago, Fort Wayne & Eastern, a single-track short line running east and west through Lima,
      Lafayette, Delphos and Elida — which is the axis the first railroad was built along, still
      carrying the name of the city it was built toward.
    </p>
    <p>
      A hundred and four more crossings in this county are on record as closed, including every one
      Conrail ever reported and every one Grand Trunk Western did. In 1906 ten fast passenger trains
      a day passed through Lima on one road alone, besides the mails and the limited express, and in
      1921 a traveller could go to bed here and wake in Chicago. The inventory records passenger
      trains per day at every crossing in the county and records zero at all of them.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>What the county sent, and what it did not get back</h2>
    <AssertionCard assertion={wentToWar} />
    <p>
      Between the canal and the oil this site had nothing. The corpus had eleven dated events and
      no Civil War in it at all, while the source sat on the shelf: sixty pages of the 1885 county
      history, of which forty-nine are roster. A county of 19,185 people put 1,920 men into United
      States service, which is one person in ten counting women, children and the old.
    </p>
    <p>
      Its war began on a Sunday morning at the second courthouse — a flag strung on a wire between
      Ashton Hall and the building, the congregations gathering under it as services ended, a store
      box for a platform. The first company was ninety-two men and was inducted eight days later.
      For a fortnight in August 1862 the county held the rendezvous of Ohio's Eighth Military
      District: about 1,600 men camped across the Ottawa River from a city of 1,989.
    </p>
    <AssertionCard assertion={warDead} />
    <ChartFigure
      id="war-dead-cause"
      title="Allen County's Civil War dead, by what the roster records"
      caption="Counted from the 1885 roster, which records each man's fate and never totals them. Every figure is the lower of two digitizations of the same page, so every figure is a floor."
      columns={['Recorded as', 'Men']}
      rows={warDead.figures.map((f) => [f.label, String(f.value)])}
    />
    <script type="application/json" data-chart-spec="war-dead-cause" set:html={JSON.stringify(causeSpec)} is:inline />
    <p>
      The largest bar is the one that says nothing. For a hundred and twenty-one of these men the
      roster gives a place and a date and no cause, and the places are hospitals and camps rather
      than battlefields. That is the ordinary arithmetic of that war, and a county roster is where
      a county keeps it.
    </p>
    <AssertionCard assertion={corinth} />
    <ChartFigure
      id="war-dead-place"
      title="Where Allen County's Civil War dead died, by named place"
      caption="A place counts only where it is named within a clause of the word recording the death, so a man who enlisted at one place and died at another is counted once. Lower of two digitizations at each place."
      columns={['Place', 'Men']}
      rows={corinth.figures.map((f) => [f.label, String(f.value)])}
    />
    <script type="application/json" data-chart-spec="war-dead-place" set:html={JSON.stringify(corinthSpec)} is:inline />
    <p>
      Three of the deaths are not military at all, and both digitizations carry them word for word:
      two men drowned, one at Hamburgh in Tennessee in 1862 and one near Fort Monroe a fortnight
      after Appomattox, and a third was lost on the <em>Sultana</em>, the steamer that burned above
      Memphis in April 1865 carrying released prisoners home. The cleaner of the two scans reads
      that year as 1805; the other reads 1865, which is the whole argument for keeping two.
    </p>
    <p>
      The three figures the county's own books give for its service — 776, 1,200 and 1,920 — are
      not in conflict, and the reason they look like it is a transmission error this corpus can
      date. The 1885 book says 776 men were traced into the roster of the 100,224 Ohio contributed
      <em>in 1861</em>. The 1906 book drops the year. The 1921 book then reports it as a whole-war
      total and sets it against an official credit of October 1863, as though a first-year cohort
      and a two-and-a-half-year credit were the same measurement. The same two books do it again
      with a sentence about the First Ohio Volunteer Infantry, which becomes a claim that Allen
      County had men on the road within sixty hours — and the 1921 book disproves itself four
      sentences later by dating the actual week, which took eight days.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>The night the jail was taken</h2>
    <AssertionCard assertion={raid} />
    <p>
      Three men came into the Allen County jail at Lima after dark on 12 October 1933, demanded
      that the prisoner be handed over, shot Sheriff Jess L. Sarber when he fought them, locked his
      wife and his deputy in the cell they had just emptied, took every gun in the building and
      drove west. The prisoner was John Dillinger. Sarber died within the hour.
    </p>
    <AssertionCard assertion={bank} />
    <p>
      That is the part usually left out of the telling, and it is the part that makes this the
      county's event rather than a national one that happened to land here. Dillinger was in the
      Allen County jail because he was charged with robbing an Allen County bank — the Commercial
      Bank at Bluffton, in the north of the same county. The night started inside it.
    </p>
    <p>
      Harry Pierpont, Charles Makley and Russell Clark were tried at Lima the following spring,
      three trials of about a week each, with Ohio National Guardsmen holding the town — machine
      gun nests at the approaches to the jail, another at the end of the cell corridor, and nobody
      admitted without a written order from the sheriff, the prosecutor or the judge. Within
      twelve months and five days of the raid, Makley had been shot dead in an escape attempt,
      Pierpont had been electrocuted and Clark was serving life.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>What it changed about this county's government</h2>
    <AssertionCard assertion={succession} />
    <p>
      The sheriff's roster on this site has shown the same two lines since the corpus first read
      it: Jess L. Sarber, 1931–1933, and then Donald F. Sarber, 1933–1935. A two-year term ending
      mid-decade, and the same surname straight after it. The roster gives no reason for either,
      and for eleven phases this corpus declined to supply one, because a record saying
      <em>died in office</em> and a guess saying <em>died in office</em> are indistinguishable once
      written down.
    </p>
    <p>
      It took a newspaper to settle it, and not the one anyone would have looked for. No Lima paper
      is digitized in the national archive — the county seat's press is simply absent — so the
      answer came from a wire story about a trial, reprinted in a Washington evening paper five
      months after the fact, which calls the murdered man "the elder Sarber, who preceded his son
      as sheriff".
    </p>
    <p>
      The other irregular line in the roster is still unexplained. James K. Everett begins and ends
      in 2017, the only holder in thirty-nine whose term does not cross a year, and the obvious
      reading — an appointment to fill a vacancy — is still only the obvious reading.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>What a tract book knows</h2>
    <p>
      The Recorder keeps a second index that nobody browses: not by name, but by ground. Every
      quarter section in the county has its own sheets, running from the federal patent forward,
      and each row is a grantor, a grantee, a volume and page, an instrument type, and two dates —
      when it was signed and when it was recorded. Four of those sheets have now been read, for the
      north-west quarter of section 17 in Bath Township, which is part of the ground under the Ford
      engine plant.
    </p>
    <p>
      This site could not say when that plant was built. It can now say that Ford Motor Company
      took the ground between August 1955 and August 1956, in five conveyances, four of them from a
      company that had spent the preceding year buying the parcels up — and that a contractor filed
      a lien for $91,546.07 against Ford on 30 September 1957 for work done on it. A lien is a
      claim for labour, so it dates a building going up in a way no directory does.
    </p>
    <p>
      The same four sheets carry two things the corpus was not looking for. The ground was a quarry
      before it was a factory, held by the National Quarries Company from 1917 and the National Lime
      &amp; Stone Company from 1944. And in September 1885, four and a half months after his well
      came in at Lima, B. C. Faurot took an oil lease over part of this quarter — the first dated
      instrument this corpus holds showing the oil boom reaching a piece of ground it can point at.
    </p>
    <p>
      About ninety-five conveyances are recorded on those four sheets between 1834 and 2003. A dozen
      of them are Ford's. The rest are a farming quarter changing hands, and this site does not
      reproduce them: the question that fetched the page chooses which rows leave it.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Two tank plants, eight years later</h2>
    <AssertionCard assertion={tanks} />
    <p>
      The county's best-known industrial fact is that it builds tanks, and the corpus could not say
      since when. It can now say something better than a date, which is that the question has two
      answers and they are not the same kind of thing. The Lima Locomotive Works — a private company
      with its works here and its offices in New York — put up a $290,000 factory building in
      February 1941 and was scheduled to begin turning out medium tanks that autumn, alongside
      Chrysler, American Locomotive, Baldwin, Pullman Standard and Pressed Steel Car.
    </p>
    <p>
      The Lima Tank Depot was something else. Still under construction in January 1943, run under
      contract by United Motors Service, Inc., it existed to receive vehicles built at arsenals all
      over the country "in a raw state of completion", fit the modifications a particular theatre
      needed, test them on its own proving ground and put them on road trains for the ports. Its
      officer in charge called it the central supply base for combat vehicles: troops in the field
      did not order from a factory, they ordered from Washington, and Washington ordered from Lima.
      Its workforce came in from thirteen counties.
    </p>
    <p>
      For two phases this site declined to say whether either of them stands where the tank plant
      stands today. Two government-owned, contractor-operated tank installations in one small Ohio
      city are very plausibly one place, and there was no source that said so.
    </p>
    <p>
      There is one now, and it says they are not. The Recorder's abstract for the ground the plant
      occupies shows it in private hands through the whole of the Second World War — mortgaged in
      1940, 1941 and 1944, leased to a fishing and game club in April 1940, crossed by pipeline
      easements its owners granted in 1939, 1940 and 1949 — and conveyed to the United States in
      five deeds between 20 and 28 June 1951, each recorded the day it was signed or the day
      after. The Depot was under construction in January 1943. A farm was standing on this ground
      when it was.
    </p>
    <p>
      So the county's tank plant is not a wartime installation at all, on this evidence. It is a
      Korean War one, bought six years after the war everyone tells the story about. The refusal
      held for two phases and was right to.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>What the war did to a locomotive builder's books</h2>
    <AssertionCard assertion={books} />
    <ChartFigure
      id="locomotive-orders"
      title="Locomotives ordered from the Lima Locomotive Works, 1939 and 1940"
      caption="Two years, reported to the county in February 1941. Not a series: the corpus holds no other year of this company's orders, and two points are a comparison rather than a trend."
      columns={['Year', 'Locomotives ordered']}
      rows={books.figures.map((f) => [f.label, f.value])}
    />
    <script type="application/json" data-chart-spec="locomotive-orders" set:html={JSON.stringify(booksSpec)} is:inline />
    <p>
      The county's largest employer went from a net loss of $134,326 to a net profit of $87,007 in
      one year, on orders that more than tripled, and booked eighteen more locomotives in the first
      seven weeks of 1941. Then it took a tank contract. Everything this corpus knows about the
      commercial side of Allen County's war comes from one page of a village weekly, which is worth
      keeping in view when reading it.
    </p>
  </section>
</Base>
